#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189379 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B)\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B$!\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y$, respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y$. Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "c151ba98-beac-55c8-9a33-b3f619f0a290" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189419 :tutorial/content "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If\n> $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)$\n> for some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.*\n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e6987fa-0133-5b85-b6a5-e58a401020b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6534 :tutorial/title "Independence When the Joint Support Is Rectangular"}]}
